<article class="env-lemma" id="0BNG">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0BNG">Lemma <span data-tag="0BNG">10.96.4</span>.</a> Let $R$ be a ring. Let $I \subset R$ be an ideal. Let $0 \to M \to N \to Q \to 0$ be an exact sequence of $R$-modules such that $Q$ is annihilated by a power of $I$. Then completion produces an exact sequence $0 \to M^\wedge \to N^\wedge \to Q \to 0$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Say $I^ c Q = 0$. Then $Q/I^ nQ = Q$ for $n \geq c$. On the other hand, it is clear that $I^ nM \subset M \cap I^ nN \subset I^{n - c}M$ for $n \geq c$. Thus $M^\wedge = \mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits M/(M \cap I^ n N)$. Apply Lemma <a href="/tag/03CA" data-tag="03CA">10.87.1</a> to the system of exact sequences 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  0 \to M/(M \cap I^ n N) \to N/I^ n N \to Q \to 0  \]
</div>
    <p>
       for $n \geq c$ to conclude. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>